{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791196520,"nonce":"eEkkc8cfdAApDVsC75bGYfTh97M=","message":"**Owner's ruling (2026-10-05), from the ADR 0054 review (F5, F2, F1).**\n1. **A personal access token may not approve a Remedy.** Approve needs a browser session, the same as the Slack-link writes. A Slack approve, a human click on a signed payload, still counts. A PAT approve is refused with a typed error. Decline stays open to PATs, because saying no cannot change a cluster.\n2. **Any member may decline.** Saying no is always safe. A declined Remedy may be proposed again by a later Investigation.\n3. **\"An approval shows the latest facts sent outbound\" (§2) is built in the UI.** The approval screen shows the Incident's last three outbound facts above its controls. In Slack the card sits in the Incident's own thread, which already shows them. §2 is restated to say so.","files":null}]}